  ],
  "text": "A rock the size of Earth should have gone cold billions of years ago. Pull a stone out of a fire tonight and it is cold by morning. Yet after four and a half billion years, our centre is still five thousand degrees. That is about as hot as the surface of the Sun, sitting under your feet. Some of that heat is simply left over from how the planet was built. Every rock that hit turned its speed into heat, and the heat had nowhere to go. Rock is a terrible conductor, so the mantle holds it in like a blanket. But leftover heat alone would have faded long ago. Something keeps topping it up. Uranium, thorium and potassium, spread through the rock, quietly decaying. About half the heat leaving the Earth today was made after the Earth was. That heat moves the continents, holds the shield, and it was never lit twice. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
